\name{gtheory_hate_speech}
\alias{gtheory_hate_speech}
\alias{hate_speech}
\docType{data}
\title{Synthetic Hate-Speech Category Judgments}
\description{An ordinal toy outcome set for illustrating repeated subjective
text-category judgments. All 576 observations are synthetic; they are not
HateXplain records, crowdworker labels, or actual LLM outputs. Access the table
with \code{\link{gt_example}}.}
\format{The returned list contains a \code{data} frame with 576 rows and six
columns. The shared panel has 24 hypothetical items, three generic evaluators,
two prompts, two temperature settings, and two seed identifiers.
\describe{
\item{item}{Factor identifying 24 hypothetical items.}
\item{evaluator, prompt, temp, seed}{See the
\link[=gtheory_datasets]{overview} for these factor levels.}
\item{score}{With native coding, an ordered factor with levels \code{1},
\code{2}, \code{3}, representing NORMAL, OFFENSIVE, and HATE. The legacy
\code{coding = "manuscript"} option returns the same values as numeric
Gaussian working scores.}
}
The remaining list elements identify the outcome family, design roles, coding,
resource path, interpretation notes, and synthetic provenance.}
\details{
The native family is ordinal with a probit link. The numeric-score option
additionally treats the category steps as equally spaced. It is a compatibility
coding of the same synthetic values, not a reproduction of a manuscript study.

The labels were generated by deterministic integer arithmetic without reading
texts, consulting annotations, or reproducing source-study prevalence. They
illustrate data format and category order only. Their completeness does not
describe any real annotation pipeline. A chosen random-source structure must
still pass the backend restrictions; use \code{\link{gt_preflight}} before fitting.
}
\source{Original synthetic observations authored by Jin Liu under GPL-3.
Public generator:
\preformatted{scripts/build_package_data.R}
See the
\link[=gtheory_datasets]{dataset overview} for the construction and provenance.
The references below provide background on rubric-conditioned annotation and
the HateXplain task. Neither supplied observations for this synthetic panel.}
\references{
Mathew, B., Saha, P., Yimam, S. M., Biemann, C., Goyal, P., and Mukherjee, A.
(2021). HateXplain: A Benchmark Dataset for Explainable Hate Speech Detection.
\emph{Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence},
35(17), 14867--14875. \doi{10.1609/aaai.v35i17.17745}.

Liu, J. (2026). Rubric-conditioned large language model labeling: Agreement,
uncertainty, and label consistency in subjective text annotation.
\emph{Computers in Human Behavior}, 181, 108988.
\doi{10.1016/j.chb.2026.108988}.
}
